{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::source_node_id` identifies the criterion's\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The node this question is about.\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> D::NodeId;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `Criterion::source_node_id` must return the criterion's source node. That is the node the criterion is evaluated on, as a `D::NodeId`. The trait declaration in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#trait[Criterion]/fn[source_node_id]` carries the `{impl}` reference. The requirement has one obligation and no failure cases, boundaries or modes.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` calls `source_node_id()` directly on a real `Review` criterion. It asserts the result equals the `HabeasNode` the criterion was built for. That checks the criterion's own method returns its source node.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` uses a test `Review` whose `source_node_id` returns its stored `source` (`Node(1)`). It asserts three things:\n  - `verify` produces a value whose `source_node_id()` is `Node(1)`.\n  - A value loaded from storage reports the same `source_node_id()` as the stored one.\n  - The stored record is keyed under that node.\n\n  This shows the node the criterion reports is the one the framework uses as the evidence's source.\n\nBoth tests carry `{test}` references to the subject, so there is no marking gap.\n\n**Possible counterexample.** An implementation could return an unrelated node, or the framework could ignore the reported node. The direct assertion in the SDK test and the `Node(1)` assertions in the contracts test would both fail in that case.\n\n**Other cited requirements.** The parent requirement `criterion_trait` and the definition of source node are context only. They need no further tests here.",
  "references": "2a0fb05f8fd461148265de0b6e203fb914e7ed9c75f88244f8ccf6a97b30f907",
